** ==============================================================================
** ABAQUS/STANDARD THERMO-METALLURGICAL MATERIAL CARD: 27MnCr5
** Case-hardening / gear steel, core composition 0.27 C - 0.25 Si - 1.25 Mn - 1.20 Cr (wt%)
** Units: mm, s, tonne, mJ, MPa, C
** Author: Victor Maia (vhfm08@gmail.com)
**
** ESTIMATED DATA FROM PUBLISHED LITERATURE. It is the 16MnCr5 / 20MnCr5 base card
** shifted with published empirical and kinetic relations for the composition of the
** crown gear steel used in Nogueira (2021), Neto (2023) and Maia (2023) (Tab. 3.1:
** C 0.23-0.31, Mn 1.10-1.40, Cr 1.00-1.40 wt%, midpoint 0.27 C - 1.25 Mn - 1.20 Cr).
**
** What changes with respect to 16MnCr5 / 20MnCr5:
**  - Ac1 = 732 C, Ac3 = 815 C, Ms = 372 C: calculated from Andrews (1965) and
**    Steven & Haynes (1956) for 0.27 C - 0.25 Si - 1.25 Mn - 1.20 Cr (Ac3 shifted
**    by -16 K and Ms by -13 K relative to the 20MnCr5 card).
**  - Solidus 1455 C (20MnCr5: 1470 C, 16MnCr5: 1485 C): linear solidus depression
**    for +0.075 wt% C; liquidus kept at 1530 C.
**  - Austenite -> ferrite (550 to 785 C): TTT times of the 20MnCr5 card multiplied
**    by the ratio 27MnCr5 / 20MnCr5 of the reaction-rate model of Li et al. (1998),
**    factor 2.3 to 6.8 between 550 and 735 C (Ae3 = 765 C); JMA k and n computed
**    directly from those TTT times (fs = 0.05, ff = 0.95, n = 2.10).
**  - Austenite -> bainite (372 to 537 C): start / end times and JMA k of the
**    Li et al. (1998) model for this composition (ASTM grain size 8), Bs = 537 C,
**    JMA exponent n = 2.11.
**  - Austenite -> martensite (below Ms = 372 C): Koistinen-Marburger law alone
**    (gamma = 0.01 1/K, ~3.1 % retained austenite at 25 C); the artificial 25 %
**    retention threshold of the guide's AISI 5140 verification input is removed.
**  - Flow curves: 16MnCr5 base curves x 1.20 (ferrite), x 1.20 (bainite),
**    x 1.255 (martensite), from the hardness ratios of the Maynier et al. (1978)
**    formulas (+9 % F, +10 % B, +15 % M against 20MnCr5); austenite unchanged.
**    The factor fades linearly to 1.0 between 800 and 1100 C.
**  - Thermal expansion: secant values from ZERO = -273.15 C rebuilt with Lement (1959)
**    specific volumes for 0.27 wt% C (F->A at 773.5 C, A->B at 537 C, A->M at 372 C)
**    and frozen above the liquidus (1530 C).
**  - Specific heat: magnetic (Curie) peak on ferrite only (~770 J/kg.K at 750 C),
**    meeting the paramagnetic austenite curve at Ac3 = 815 C (647 J/kg.K).
**  - Heating transformations: ferrite / bainite / martensite -> austenite end at
**    Ac3 = 815 C, with log-linear interpolation at 900, 1000, 1200 C up to 1455 C.
**
** References:
**  - L. F. J. Nogueira, MSc thesis, Univ. Coimbra (2021), hdl.handle.net/10316/97996
**  - A. A. R. Neto, MSc thesis, Univ. Aveiro (2023), hdl.handle.net/10773/41648
**  - V. H. F. Maia, MSc thesis, Univ. Aveiro (2023), hdl.handle.net/10773/39345
**  - R. Kawulok et al., Arch. Metall. Mater. 63 (2018) 1735-1741, doi:10.24425/amm.2018.125099
**  - M. V. Li, D. V. Niebuhr, L. L. Meekisho, D. G. Atteridge, Metall. Mater. Trans. B 29 (1998) 661-672
**  - K. W. Andrews, J. Iron Steel Inst. 203 (1965) 721-727
**  - W. Steven, A. G. Haynes, J. Iron Steel Inst. 183 (1956) 349-359
**  - P. Maynier, B. Jungmann, J. Dollet, Hardenability Concepts with Applications to Steel, AIME (1978) 518-545
**  - D. P. Koistinen, R. E. Marburger, Acta Metall. 7 (1959) 59-60
**  - B. S. Lement, Distortion in Tool Steels, ASM (1959)
**  - EN ISO 683-3 (heat-treatable / case-hardening steels)
